  ImageSize->Full]], "Output",
 CellFrame->False,
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"66caf597-71f0-4285-a075-f4d58da04619"],

Cell["\<\
Figure 4. Empirical region count vs hidden width tracks the Zaslavsky bound \
at slope 2 on log-log axes. Trained networks realise roughly 0.5-0.95 of the \
bound -- they do not saturate the maximum arrangement but they do live in the \
same asymptotic class.\
\>", "Text",
 TextAlignment->Center,
 FontFamily->"Helvetica",
 FontSize->11,
 FontSlant->Italic,ExpressionUUID->"17a9804e-5246-4dd7-bdc8-ac32bfb331e2"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The slope-2 fit is genuine ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SuperscriptBox["n", "2"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "45914f33-5070-4500-997c-ccf8d6383b8c"],
 " scaling. Doubling the hidden width roughly quadruples the expressivity, \
measured in linear regions. For deeper networks (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["L",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "bff3fae6-5919-45c2-90db-ef9832d50656"],
 " layers of width ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["n",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "ad6d46a8-74d6-4dba-923a-d0bec290f529"],
 "), the same tropical analysis gives an ",
 StyleBox["upper bound",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " of order ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SuperscriptBox["n", 
      RowBox[{"2", " ", "L"}]],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "e61189ed-bc5c-49bf-b6b7-5ca9214e63aa"],
 " regions for 2D input -- exponential in depth -- which is the classical \
\"why depth helps\" story. The catch: trained networks rarely come anywhere \
near that maximum. Hanin and Rolnick (2019) proved that for typical (random \
or trained) weights the ",
 StyleBox["expected",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " number of linear regions grows only ",
 StyleBox["polynomially",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " in the total neuron count, not exponentially in depth. Depth is a \
force-multiplier on potential expressivity, not on the realised count -- and \
the trained networks in our \[Section]16 sweep (which sit at 0.5-0.95 of the \
Zaslavsky ceiling at fixed depth one) are typical rather than exceptional in \
that respect."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5b1b9fb8-ddb0-4b07-8b53-f714221f731a"],

Cell["17.  A note on Maxout", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"f50d3ecd-9f04-4e67-8f2b-4d3385f5c23a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "If we replace the ReLU layer with a maxout layer of group size ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["k",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "006a7f2d-d6da-40e8-8691-38937fa59090"],
 ", the hidden layer itself is already a vector of tropical polynomials by \
construction -- no positive / negative weight split needed inside the \
activation. The full network's output, however, is still ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"c", ".", "h"}], "+", "d"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "568e718e-db98-4717-92d4-3d95e230e381"],
 " for some output weight vector ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["c",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "d605028a-6397-45f2-a7bb-9fa0c4464f03"],
 ", so the P/Q decomposition continues to depend on the sign of the \
output-layer weights. ",
 StyleBox["When all output weights are non-negative",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " the network becomes a ",
 StyleBox["pure tropical polynomial",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " -- ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["P",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "cda8004e-ae40-4735-b277-ebb2114abf62"],
 " alone, no ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["Q",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "588c9b7c-4eda-4993-ae23-933c6d1a162f"],
 " -- and then the cells of the regular subdivision of its single Newton \
polytope correspond bijectively to the realised linear regions; the decision \
boundary is a single tropical hypersurface; and the analytical tools below \
sharpen dramatically. With mixed-sign output weights the same P/Q machinery \
as for ReLU is needed; the only thing that changes is the per-monomial \
bookkeeping inside each hidden unit."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"df4aa9c4-660c-40c0-98b2-b7e0413f89b3"],

Cell["18.  Beyond standard papers", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"15ddcbe3-9093-4868-aae7-a76f9013f810"],

Cell["\<\
The tropical viewpoint gives us three concrete capabilities that go beyond \
the standard \"ReLU networks are tropical rationals\" literature. Each is \
implementable in a few lines on top of the WL machinery already developed, \
and each is genuinely useful.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5855ea01-299e-4e26-b270-624c47f81eb9"],

Cell["18.1  Lipschitz constant via region enumeration", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"40ef34ea-d7af-43e3-add3-48d655513bee"],

Cell["\<\
Standard upper bounds on the Lipschitz constant of a ReLU network multiply \
layer-wise norms and become loose -- often by a large factor. The tropical \
viewpoint replaces that bound with a direct computation: the maximum gradient \
norm over realised activation patterns.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e4735449-5653-41dd-a768-9050ef937a28"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Reason: the trained network is piecewise-affine. On the open region of \
input space with activation pattern \[Sigma], the network is the single \
affine function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"f", "(", "x", ")"}], "\[LongEqual]", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        SubscriptBox["w", "\[Sigma]"], ".", "x"}], "+", 
       SubscriptBox["d", "\[Sigma]"]}]}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "103545cd-c969-40dc-8e04-f1b8c3d5765c"],
 " whose gradient is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["w", "\[Sigma]"], "\[LongEqual]", 
      RowBox[{
       UnderscriptBox["\"\<\[Sum]\>\"", "\"\<j with \[Sigma]_j = 1\>\""], " ", 
       SubscriptBox["c", "j"], " ", 
       SubscriptBox["a", "j"]}]}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "9488b27c-b771-4517-bad2-2fd37350b8c8"],
 ". The Lipschitz constant of the whole function is therefore the maximum of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     TemplateBox[{
       SubscriptBox["w", "\[Sigma]"]},
      "Norm"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "74d6df35-1fac-49ec-ada6-2a0d11b41ddc"],
 " over realised activation patterns. We enumerate them on a fine grid, \
compute the gradient on each, and take the max:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0a70be6f-0df8-4722-ba66-6f31ba8cf14f"],

Cell["\<\
dom   = {{-2., 2.}, {-2., 2.}};
lipschitz = MPExactLipschitz[<|\"A\" -> A, \"b\" -> b, \"c\" -> c,
                              \"d\" -> d|>, dom, 240];

{lipschitz[\"Lipschitz\"],
 lipschitz[\"Patterns\"],
 lipschitz[\"ArgMaxPattern\"]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"b9c19a45-f252-46c2-98ba-694ab9ef1f92"],

Cell[BoxData[
 PaneBox[
  RowBox[{"{", 
   RowBox[{"5.613367900945316`", ",", "72", ",", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
     "1", ",", "1", ",", "1", ",", "1", ",", "0", ",", "0", ",", "0", ",", 
      "1", ",", "0", ",", "0", ",", "0", ",", "1"}], "}"}]}], "}"}],
  Alignment->{Center, Top},
  ImageSize->Full]], "Output",
 CellFrame->False,
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"8a044cd6-1f0e-41a9-9668-00c05853a02f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Caveat: this is a grid-sampled enumeration, not an exact combinatorial one. \
With 12 hidden units the 12 crease lines partition ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SuperscriptBox["R", "2"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "8652633c-fd30-4781-a36e-d38ebc99a946"],
 " into at most ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["lipZaslavsky",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "bb36938f-7907-4b15-8172-06705cb16f2b"],
 " linear regions (Zaslavsky bound, \[Section]16). Our 240\[Times]240 grid \
discovered ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"lipRes", "(", "\"\<Patterns\>\"", ")"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "048597fa-bafe-45d2-bee7-4fca4d853b77"],
 " realised patterns -- close to that ceiling, and stable under refinement -- \
so we expect every linear region of the trained network to be hit. For a \
strict certification one should enumerate the arrangement exactly; the grid \
result is the best ",
 StyleBox["finite",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " lower bound and is tight whenever the grid is fine enough."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e1d74c5f-e0d5-4856-80f8-c042999d5097"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The grid-enumerated Lipschitz constant of the trained moons classifier on \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SuperscriptBox[
      RowBox[{"Times", "[", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"-", "2"}], ",", "2"}], "}"}], "]"}], "2"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "b9ad9ee7-57e5-46a9-b183-9d0e15e456a2"],
 " is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"lipRes", "(", "\"\<Lipschitz\>\"", ")"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "120a5ecf-09f6-4690-9a7a-f520664a2072"],
 ", attained on the linear region with activation pattern ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"lipRes", "(", "\"\<ArgMaxPattern\>\"", ")"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "6f4db616-b01e-46b4-9fda-a94b4c03d1d3"],
 " (these specific hidden units are simultaneously active). Now compare to \
the standard product-of-layer-norms upper bound ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{
      TemplateBox[{"c"},
       "Norm"], " ", 
      RowBox[{"SubscriptBox", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        TemplateBox[{"A"},
         "Norm"], ",", "2"}], "]"}]}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "9ce50208-e3fe-4830-b2c8-26c041c1d19e"],
 " (Norm[A, 2] is the spectral norm of A, i.e. its largest singular value):"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d9c865c4-b3f3-4be9-9f11-47db4b9e5bf9"],

Cell["\<\
chainBound = Norm[c] * Norm[A, 2];
{
  \"\\[ScriptCapitalL] (grid)        \" -> lipschitz[\"Lipschitz\"],
  \"||c|| * ||A||_op  \"               -> chainBound,
  \"chain / grid      \"               -> chainBound / lipschitz[\"Lipschitz\"]
} // TableForm\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"5edd9d50-736c-486e-9e86-9d3673cb952f"],

Cell[BoxData[
 PaneBox[
  TagBox[
   TagBox[GridBox[{
      {
       RowBox[{"\<\"\[ScriptCapitalL] (grid)        \"\>", "\[Rule]", 
        "5.613367900945316`"}]},
      {
       RowBox[{"\<\"||c|| * ||A||_op  \"\>", "\[Rule]", 
        "13.731892367845129`"}]},
      {
       RowBox[{"\<\"chain / grid      \"\>", "\[Rule]", "2.446284050887279`"}]}
     },
     GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}, "Rows" -> {{Baseline}}},
     GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {
         Offset[0.27999999999999997`], {
          Offset[0.5599999999999999]}, 
         Offset[0.27999999999999997`]}, "Rows" -> {
         Offset[0.2], {
          Offset[0.4]}, 
         Offset[0.2]}}],
    Column],
   Function[BoxForm`e$, 
    TableForm[BoxForm`e$]]],
  Alignment->{Center, Top},
  ImageSize->Full]], "Output",
 CellFrame->False,
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"4339b6db-97a0-4e17-94c8-17607501eeea"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The chain-rule bound is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"N", "[", 
      RowBox[{"lipRatio", ",", "3"}], "]"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "c1f2eba1-a9b6-4ba3-a973-02a99abe1a66"],
 "\[Times] larger than the value we get from the actual gradient enumeration. \
For deeper networks the gap grows multiplicatively per layer -- which is \
exactly why product-of-layer-norms is famously slack. The enumerated value is \
what you want for certified adversarial robustness and for sharp \
generalisation bounds."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6f61a2ce-6135-40b1-8c69-2bbd67f7b5aa"],

Cell["18.2  Dead-neuron detection", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"174536bf-0bf9-453c-be60-4286f210f945"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A common failure mode of ReLU training is ",
 StyleBox["dead neurons",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ": units whose pre-activation never goes positive on the data manifold, so \
the unit always outputs zero and never gets gradient. The standard detection \
method is to evaluate on a held-out set and check whether the unit ever \
fires. The tropical viewpoint gives a zero-data alternative."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f008cf25-3609-4aeb-8b7d-041de31a9d8b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Each unit's pre-activation ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["a", "j"], ".", "x"}], "+", 
      SubscriptBox["b", "j"]}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "2befc568-26e6-4fae-9270-a57a64999c0e"],
 " is an affine function. Its maximum on an axis-aligned rectangle is \
attained at one of the four corners. So we can ",
 StyleBox["read off",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " whether the unit is dead on a given input domain by evaluating at the \
corners:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ea8fb749-36a1-403c-9522-7a0bcf3365f0"],

Cell["\<\
netAssoc = <|\"A\" -> A, \"b\" -> b, \"c\" -> c, \"d\" -> d|>;
{
  \"dead on [-2, 2]^2\"      -> MPDeadUnits[netAssoc, {{-2, 2}, {-2, 2}}],
  \"dead on [-0.5, 0.5]^2\"  -> MPDeadUnits[netAssoc, {{-.5, .5}, {-.5, .5}}]
} // TableForm\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"ed98d922-d3ae-46eb-b6dc-b7fead882e54"],

Cell[BoxData[
 PaneBox[
  TagBox[
   TagBox[GridBox[{
      {
       RowBox[{"\<\"dead on [-2, 2]^2\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
        RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"\<\"NeverActive\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
           RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ",", 
          RowBox[{"\<\"AlwaysActive\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
           RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}]}], "\[RightAssociation]"}]}]},
      {
       RowBox[{"\<\"dead on [-0.5, 0.5]^2\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
        RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"\<\"NeverActive\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
           RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ",", 
          RowBox[{"\<\"AlwaysActive\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
           RowBox[{"{", "2", "}"}]}]}], "\[RightAssociation]"}]}]}
     },
     GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}, "Rows" -> {{Baseline}}},
     GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {
         Offset[0.27999999999999997`], {
          Offset[0.5599999999999999]}, 
         Offset[0.27999999999999997`]}, "Rows" -> {
         Offset[0.2], {
          Offset[0.4]}, 
         Offset[0.2]}}],
    Column],
   Function[BoxForm`e$, 
    TableForm[BoxForm`e$]]],
  Alignment->{Center, Top},
  ImageSize->Full]], "Output",
 CellFrame->False,
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"71938f91-3e0d-479a-ae33-917b723be7bf"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "On the full plot rectangle, no unit of the trained moons network is dead. \
On the smaller box ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SuperscriptBox[
      RowBox[{"Times", "[", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"-", "0.5`"}], ",", "0.5`"}], "}"}], "]"}], "2"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "72960f0e-959b-4987-8124-d52da876c088"],
 " around the origin, however, unit ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"First", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"deadSmall", "(", "\"\<AlwaysActive\>\"", ")"}], 
       ",", "\"\<none\>\""}], "]"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "a455a6fc-b547-4f59-824b-f1de670d8d47"],
 " is ",
 StyleBox["always active",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " -- its ReLU is just the affine pre-activation. We can therefore ",
 StyleBox["fold that unit into the output bias",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " on this domain and save one ReLU evaluation per forward pass."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f3e2cad6-66ce-4b64-8821-7c40c9d81dfc"],

Cell["18.3  Lossless minimisation", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"372a34c5-3dea-483d-a587-88ec5aa187ea"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Combining the two observations above gives a ",
 StyleBox["zero-retraining network pruner",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " that is guaranteed exact on any bounded domain. The recipe: drop the \
never-active units (they contribute zero), and absorb the always-active units \
into an affine head (their ReLU is the identity on the domain of interest). \
The resulting smaller network -- plus a constant linear correction stored in \
the returned association as ",
 StyleBox["AffineHead",
  FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
  Background->GrayLevel[0.95]],
 " -- computes ",
 StyleBox["exactly",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " the same function as the original on the chosen domain. No approximation, \
no fidelity-vs-size trade-off."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"137b8255-925f-4291-bdb5-1f1fb96b9c9e"],

Cell["\<\
pruned = MPPruneDeadUnits[netAssoc, {{-0.5, 0.5}, {-0.5, 0.5}}];
{pruned[\"OriginalUnits\"], pruned[\"KeptUnits\"],
 pruned[\"AffineHead\", \"Slope\"]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"c5b1d438-4a9a-40ba-a1ff-1e0690e185c2"],

Cell[BoxData[
 PaneBox[
  RowBox[{"{", 
   RowBox[{"12", ",", "11", ",", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"0.5180888218354163`", ",", 
      RowBox[{"-", "0.005519689277882905`"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}],
  Alignment->{Center, Top},
  ImageSize->Full]], "Output",
 CellFrame->False,
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"fb6a021b-a197-49a1-a320-01bdbca6a0d7"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "On the smaller domain, the 12-unit moons network is equivalent to an \
11-unit network plus a small affine head ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["w", "\"\<head\>\""], ".", "x"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "e83a2043-4cf2-499d-abfa-6fc0abdd98d3"],
 " with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SubscriptBox["w", "\"\<head\>\""],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "ed4c9da0-40a5-48b1-a646-96e04d2dd7ec"],
 " = ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"pruneSmall", "(", 
      RowBox[{"\"\<AffineHead\>\"", ",", "\"\<Slope\>\""}], ")"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "e44d949a-c319-4ae7-a43e-f345d076aff7"],
 ". ",
 StyleBox["ReLUForward",
  FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
  Background->GrayLevel[0.95]],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["MaxPlusForward",
  FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
  Background->GrayLevel[0.95]],
 " consult the ",
 StyleBox["AffineHead",
  FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
  Background->GrayLevel[0.95]],
 " field automatically, so the pruned association is a drop-in replacement. \
The next cell verifies losslessness inline on 500 random inputs in the \
pruning domain:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"18e11476-aadb-4128-9380-27b0dc34bf73"],

Cell["\<\
SeedRandom[42];
testPts = RandomReal[{-0.5, 0.5}, {500, 2}];
{
  \"max |f(x) - f_pruned(x)|       (classical) \" ->
    Max[Abs[ReLUForward[netAssoc, #] & /@ testPts -
            ReLUForward[pruned,   #] & /@ testPts]],
  \"max |f_pruned(x) - (P-Q)(x)|   (tropical)  \" ->
    Max[Abs[ReLUForward[pruned,   #] & /@ testPts -
            MaxPlusForward[pruned, #] & /@ testPts]]
} // TableForm\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"5accec1b-448f-47ba-aa81-1be9854218fb"],

Cell[BoxData[
 PaneBox[
  TagBox[
   TagBox[GridBox[{
      {
       RowBox[{"\<\"max |f(x) - f_pruned(x)|       (classical) \"\>", "\[Rule]",
         "4.440892098500626`*^-16"}]},
      {
       RowBox[{"\<\"max |f_pruned(x) - (P-Q)(x)|   (tropical)  \"\>", "\[Rule]",
         "6.661338147750939`*^-16"}]}
     },
     GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}, "Rows" -> {{Baseline}}},
     GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {
         Offset[0.27999999999999997`], {
          Offset[0.5599999999999999]}, 
         Offset[0.27999999999999997`]}, "Rows" -> {
         Offset[0.2], {
          Offset[0.4]}, 
         Offset[0.2]}}],
    Column],
   Function[BoxForm`e$, 
    TableForm[BoxForm`e$]]],
  Alignment->{Center, Top},
  ImageSize->Full]], "Output",
 CellFrame->False,
 TextAlignment->Center,ExpressionUUID->"bb13a1ee-083f-48ee-9309-8bb7ced91fef"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Both errors are at floating-point round-off (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"Order", "[", 
      FractionBox["1", 
       SuperscriptBox["10", "16"]], "]"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "4012a1d3-2e1e-475d-b6ec-7a15b31f45df"],
 "). The reduction is ",
 StyleBox["lossless",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " on this domain -- the pruned net computes pointwise the same function as \
the original, both in the classical ReLU pipeline and in the tropical ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"P", "-", "Q"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "717625b6-46a9-424e-bf00-2d5d7b46ec74"],
 " evaluation."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6bfe559e-c716-424e-95ba-46fcb7029469"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For a wider trained network (say 64 or 128 hidden units), the fraction of \
always-active or never-active units typically grows. The minimiser above \
gives a principled, exact way to ",
 StyleBox["shrink",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " the network without retraining and without measurable loss of behaviour on \
the operating domain -- a fundamentally different mechanism from standard \
distillation or quantization, both of which trade fidelity for size."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2808ba36-8a86-44d7-a4f3-1a5c7b557c11"],

Cell["What else falls out", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"829dc837-e0ac-44b0-90ad-67ce830a2af9"],

Cell["\<\
Several other results follow immediately from the same picture; we sketch \
them for completeness:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a9c4df93-92f7-4d61-b5ed-d5e98c75228b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Exact symbolic decision boundary",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": each realised activation pattern \[Sigma] yields one affine piece of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["f",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "142e0ae3-fca0-438c-8040-46d094f490e5"],
 "; the decision boundary ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"f", "\[LongEqual]", "0"}], "}"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "a74fd6ba-468d-45ec-ae67-915b29cbd3d4"],
 " is the union of line segments where adjacent pieces switch sign, with \
explicit equations.\n",
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Adversarial certification",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": for an input ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SubscriptBox["x", "0"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "88f6c58a-a758-4001-ac47-be6f20ddd096"],
 " the largest radius ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["r",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "b10da937-02ee-4967-b9b7-2432e37b8b41"],
 " of an attack ball around ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SubscriptBox["x", "0"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "3d44586a-0359-4d2b-8282-5af0648fd91a"],
 " on which the classifier output sign is constant is the (norm-specific) \
distance from ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SubscriptBox["x", "0"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "5047f14b-ff4e-4cae-84fd-4da24ddef2aa"],
 " to the nearest segment of the decision boundary ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"f", "\[LongEqual]", "0"}], "}"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "3ad543e3-5dff-486b-bbf7-959c2643ec73"],
 ". For a region with gradient ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SubscriptBox["w", "\[Sigma]"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "d0df67e3-75de-4288-92d4-4a310f37b30f"],
 " and offset ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SubscriptBox["d", "\[Sigma]"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "7ef26418-a4d9-46f0-800b-97f0ac337d2a"],
 " the analytic distance from ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SubscriptBox["x", "0"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "b8339805-92c8-4403-a697-b3212a5b6362"],
 " to that piece of the hyperplane is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     FractionBox[
      TemplateBox[{
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox["w", "\[Sigma]"], ".", 
            SubscriptBox["x", "0"]}], "+", 
          SubscriptBox["d", "\[Sigma]"]}]},
       "Abs"], 
      SubscriptBox[
       TemplateBox[{
         SubscriptBox["w", "\[Sigma]"]},
        "Norm"], "\"\<\[Star]\>\""]],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "905d9705-576d-4f8e-b9d8-c2612ad3d413"],
 ", where the \[Star] subscript on the norm denotes the ",
 StyleBox["dual norm",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " of the attack norm (an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SuperscriptBox["L", "2"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "d3aa455e-d642-47af-999d-eaf67ed85498"],
 " ball gives the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SuperscriptBox["L", "2"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "bdecf0a7-c3f3-409b-8658-8455760781de"],
 " norm in the denominator; an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SuperscriptBox["L", "\[Infinity]"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "3e194cc3-4de6-4e87-a9b2-ea827b9ea7ae"],
 " ball gives the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SuperscriptBox["L", "1"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "ece38c59-4554-4b11-97b0-090fc7828e2c"],
 " norm; etc.). Minimise over realised patterns \[Sigma] and clip to each \
region's own polytope to get the certified radius.\n",
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Network equivalence",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": if two trained networks have tropical decompositions whose upper hulls \
coincide in both ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["P",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "151480f6-d096-4597-bba3-5fb6075e2754"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["Q",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "b2f56a29-b11f-47cf-a666-aa6062fba9e0"],
 " then they compute the same function. The converse is not automatic -- \
dominated monomials below the upper hull can differ between two networks that \
compute the same piecewise-linear function, so the test is sufficient, not \
necessary. Still much sharper than comparing weights, because many weight \
assignments give the same upper hull.\n",
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Maxout regularity bonus",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": when a maxout network's output layer has all non-negative weights the \
entire machinery sharpens -- the network is a pure tropical polynomial (no ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     FractionBox["P", "Q"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "934daf95-48d3-413b-825d-e5c30f2e4188"],
 " split), and the cells of the regular subdivision of its single Newton \
polytope correspond bijectively to the realised regions. With mixed \
output-weight signs the same P/Q analysis as for ReLU applies."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8e58ab69-4bcb-436d-ba4f-9a542e75954f"],

Cell["19.  Putting it together", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"c95fdb6d-cad5-4306-8f8d-fb50c8c043ab"],

Cell["The whole story compresses into one table:", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0c3d4d15-b7cc-4831-9e04-3601f0f08757"],

